  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the conception and experimental realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B011",
    "B045"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B014 remains my preferred achievement. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea solved a central practical problem in exploiting many trapped atoms without compromising clock accuracy, and its experimental development has produced a reproducible precision-measurement platform. The result is mature not merely in record performance but in systematic control, independent realization, and its use for timekeeping, geodesy, and tests of fundamental physics. Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's defining experimental advances give a compact and credible two-person attribution.",
    "case_against": "The optical-clock revolution has depended on a wider international community, so the citation must be confined to the lattice-clock concept and its decisive realization rather than claim credit for all optical-clock metrology. Detailed historical review should also test the boundaries between the named work and other important clock pioneers.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED is a mature and foundational controllable light-matter platform, while noting both the possible relevance of Steven M. Girvin and overlap with already recognized superconducting-circuit advances.",
        "response": "I agree that circuit QED is experimentally profound and central to quantum technology. The attribution problem is nevertheless material: its theory and experimental architecture involve more essential figures than a compact slate comfortably captures, and the boundary to previously recognized superconducting-circuit physics needs sharper definition. B014 has a more discrete experimental principle and a clearer attribution at present."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson emphasizes that neodymium-iron-boron magnets are industrially indispensable, fully mature, and unusually clearly associated with Masato Sagawa and John J. Croat.",
        "response": "The enduring technological impact of B022 is compelling, and its independent discovery routes are an important strength. I would still place B014 ahead because it created a new precision-measurement capability whose physical reach is expanding, while B022 requires especially careful separation of the original materials discovery from subsequent alloy and manufacturing development."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth regards the Cirac-Zoller trapped-ion programme as a clear theoretical foundation extensively realized in experiment, while recognizing its place within a broader quantum-information landscape.",
        "response": "I agree that B018 has unusually coherent theoretical attribution and strong experimental vindication. My qualification is that the proposed achievement is principally a theoretical roadmap whose recognition must be separated from many experimental pioneers; the lattice clocks provide a more tightly bounded combination of physical concept, realization, and demonstrated metrological consequence."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical evidence supports limiting the optical-lattice-clock prize to Katori and Ye while adequately distinguishing other foundational optical-clock contributors.",
      "Whether the citation should explicitly include the magic-wavelength principle or retain broader wording that does not overstate the scope of the award."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
